Upstream-bound: no
Subject: [PORT_HOST] P6.32 phase 1: the new full-screen map screen opens from the Map screen (Square)

P6.32 (docs/P6.32_MAP_ZOOM.md, option D). STSTATUS_updateMapScreen, in TASK_RUN,
calls mapViewFrame, which gathers what the port screen needs and hands it to
dw3_port_map_view_frame (port/map_view/map_view.c): the map's sprite sheet, the
file and game half, the home mark, the visited flags, the 47 spot positions, the
reveal-layer pieces, the logical pad (held and pressed masks) and the game's own
cursor and scroll. Square opens the new host-drawn screen; the cancel button
(the PS-side Triangle bit, so it follows a button remap) closes it, Square does
not. While it is open the function returns 1: the game's screen skips its own
pad read (so the Triangle that closes the new screen does not also close the
Map), and only draws its frozen frame underneath. Nothing is written to GAME or
to the task except the cursor and scroll below, which are put back when the new
screen closes. With the option file key map_screen off (the default since phase
1), widescreen off, or without the native overlay the function returns 0 and
nothing changes: the Map screen is the one of patch 0080 (0 differing pixels,
replay us/eu-map-screen).

HUD elements (owner, after preview.45): the full-screen view shows the game's own
balloon, cursor and name box. While it is open the pad (and the left stick)
moves the view's cursor over the whole map; mapViewFrame sets the game's cursor
and scroll to it every frame (cursorX/Y = its place on the 320x240 menu picture,
the scroll so that cursor - scroll is the map position) and runs
STSTATUS_showMapArea on it, so the area lookup, the confirm sound, the name
window and its text are the game's, drawn into the frozen game picture; the host
leaves the name box's rectangle to those pixels. The animation frames of the
cursor and of the home mark, and the bounds of the name box frame, go to the
host through Dw3MapViewIo. The game's cursor and scroll are put back when the
new screen closes; phase 1 also hands them to the host at every call, so the log
can say the Map screen came back unchanged.

